# muni architecture

## Runtime topology

```text
Browser (React + Tiptap + Yjs + IndexedDB)
  ├─ REST / SSE ───────────────┐
  └─ WebSocket CRDT/awareness ─┤
                               ▼
                    muni Go modular monolith
                      ├─ auth / OIDC
                      ├─ document / revision / ACL
                      ├─ comments / suggestions / workflow
                      ├─ collaboration relay
                      ├─ AI gateway
                      ├─ REST / MCP / export
                      └─ settings / key management / audit
                               │
                               ▼
                         PostgreSQL 15+
```

React assets, Korean web fonts, Go API, the OOXML reader/writer, the pure-Go PDF text extractor and the headless Chromium used for PDF rendering are all present in `muni:v<version>`.

Presentations are generated by Ptium, not by muni. The two services talk over
REST only: reaching into Ptium's database would tie muni to a data model it does
not own and break it on every change there. muni stores the link — document,
revision, presentation id, status — and nothing else, because the deck can be
edited in Ptium afterwards and a stored copy would immediately disagree with it.

The adapter reads the ProseMirror tree rather than a flattened string. A brief
that says "these three lines are steps" or "this figure is a measure" lets a
generator reach for a process diagram or a KPI row; one handed prose has to
re-derive that structure and usually settles for bullets. The brief is not
muni-shaped, so another document source can be added later without changing
anything downstream.

